From 15b82d903f07d402ee4dd103977f4b474fd04e5b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OmarAfet <135471287+OmarAfet@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 22:02:52 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] Leave diagnostic-only span positions out of the public
 API hash

The public API hash decides whether a dependent crate has to be compiled again. It included the positions of all spans in the metadata, so adding a line at the top of a file changed the hash of every item below it, and all dependents were compiled again, even though nothing they compile changed.

Most spans in the metadata are only read by dependents to report diagnostics: bounds, generics, attributes, identifiers, module children, trait definitions. Tables of those are now hashed with `RDRHashAllDiagnosticSpans`, which hashes each span by its file and syntax context, and collects the positions in a separate hash. `def_span` positions stay in the public hash only when they can reach downstream machine code (`def_span_reaches_codegen`): debug info of MIR that dependents instantiate, and shims they generate. `mir_for_ctfe` bodies keep their positions when const evaluation may turn them into values through the `caller_location` intrinsic, and for constructors, which are also code generated. Without codegen (`--emit=metadata`, as `cargo check` uses), no position reaches machine code.

The positions left out are stored as `span_positions_hash` in the metadata, and every query that reads another crate now also depends on the `public_span_positions_hash` query, so incremental compilation of dependents still notices moved spans, for example in cached diagnostics.

A span whose position is left out is hashed in place instead of being recorded as a node of the reachability graph, whose node hashes include positions.
